{"connection_id": "BC-9f3ec6e11c52fc4a5a53", "ref": "Irenaeus, Against Heresies 5.15.2", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Christ's clay healing and primal formation", "note": "Irenaeus reads Jesus' clay healing of the man born blind as the Creator's original art of forming humanity from earth being displayed and restored. This patristic reading makes bodily formation and repair central to resurrection faith."}
{"connection_id": "BC-87f2add9168f4adf520d", "ref": "Peshitta Luke 4:16", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Syriac q-r-ʾ, read aloud", "note": "The Syriac Peshitta uses the cognate q-r-ʾ verb when Jesus stands to read Isaiah; this is a specific scriptural reading use beside 87:6's recitation, while the commentary's separate womb-related branch should not be transferred to this verse."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b3aa261933e5823fb8f1", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.13.11", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "childhood put away on becoming an adult", "note": "Paul contrasts speaking and thinking as a child with becoming an adult; this gives the commentary's child-to-maturity sequence a distinct ethical and interpretive use."}
{"connection_id": "BC-21b9bf2a3532a78ab91d", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.15.35", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "question of resurrection body", "note": "Paul explicitly asks how the dead are raised and with what sort of body they come. This names the bodily-resurrection question that the commentary raises through Q 75:40."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f36aa526adb5d793cc12", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.15.36", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "seed must die to be made alive", "note": "Paul's seed analogy makes death the condition for new life; beside the commentary's embryo-to-resurrection argument and its formed-body/drying-plant pairing, it joins plant and bodily renewal."}
{"connection_id": "BC-384d11be687595fa3034", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.15.36", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "seed dies before new life", "note": "Paul's seed image says death precedes new life. It provides a vegetal pattern for the commentary's move from a dying pasture and mortal body toward life beyond death."}
{"connection_id": "BC-afb24db1c53af8d52ce1", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.15.37", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "bare grain and body to come", "note": "The sower does not sow the body that will come, but a bare grain. This distinguishes initial material from its later formed body, a strong analogue to the commentary's unformed and completed stages."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6bf23fba85c41e616abd", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.15.38", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God gives each seed its body as willed", "note": "Paul says God gives each seed its own body as he wills, a close specific counterpart to the commentary's measured and specially formed body under 87:3."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9f4d6d83fb3de8333632", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.15.38", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "God gives each seed its body", "note": "God gives the seed a body as he chooses, to each seed its own body. The sentence makes divine assignment of bodily form an explicit resurrection counterpart to creating and proportioning."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8a1132c05025d47b332c", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.15.42", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "perishable sown, imperishable raised", "note": "Paul's contrast between a perishable body sown and an imperishable body raised directly answers the commentary's link between bodily creation and resurrection."}
{"connection_id": "BC-96300c3f81d6c8e36f0d", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.15.42", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "perishable body raised imperishable", "note": "Paul contrasts a perishable sown body with an imperishable raised one. It makes the commentary's mortality-and-afterlife warning bodily and eschatological."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5c470ea53a4cffa8ed0d", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.15.43", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "weakness raised in power", "note": "The body is sown in weakness and raised in power. This supplies an eschatological reversal of the frailty and declining strength that the commentary associates with old age."}
{"connection_id": "BC-84f3dd84787418cfd308", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.15.44", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "natural body and spiritual body", "note": "Paul distinguishes the body sown as natural from the body raised as spiritual, extending the commentary's formed body and resurrection connection into a claim about transformation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9015a836866d88626d5d", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.15.44", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "natural and spiritual body", "note": "Paul distinguishes a natural body from a raised spiritual body, without discarding bodiliness. It is a useful Christian development of the commentary's body-plus-breath sequence."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6863d686661be25d7a1b", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.15.45", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "first Adam living soul, last Adam life-giving spirit", "note": "Paul interprets Genesis 2:7 by contrasting the first Adam as living soul with the last Adam as life-giving spirit; this links the commentary's formed human and breathed life to resurrection through a Christian reading of Adam."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6772578508da3f130066", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.15.45", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "living soul and life-giving Spirit", "note": "Paul rereads Adam's becoming a living soul against the last Adam as life-giving Spirit. This Christian interpretation directly develops the formed-and-breathed human of Genesis into an eschatological body-and-life contrast."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4486494c91cde2b1b888", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.15.49", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "bearing the heavenly image", "note": "Paul says believers will bear the image of the heavenly one. It continues his bodily-resurrection argument by moving from an earthly formed humanity toward a transformed eschatological embodiment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ec362572eac0d0986022", "ref": "SBLGNT:1John.2.17", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "world passes, God's will abides", "note": "John says the world and its desire pass away while the one doing God's will abides forever; this closely matches the surah's worldly-life versus better, enduring hereafter contrast."}
{"connection_id": "BC-665f3761689493f3bd0c", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.1.23", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "new birth through imperishable seed and word", "note": "Peter says his readers have been born anew through imperishable seed by God's living word; this meets the commentary's womb-and-recitation cluster and introduces a Christian new-birth image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7fb0d4449e8abadd4719", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.1.23", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "born again through enduring word", "note": "First Peter describes a new birth through imperishable seed and enduring word. It offers a Christian extension from bodily birth to lasting life, anticipating the flesh-and-grass contrast in the next verse."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6172781f44bc95b52661", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.1.24", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "all flesh like grass", "note": "Peter quotes Isaiah that all flesh and its glory are like grass and flower; this directly joins the commentary's mortal body and withering pasture."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3d3e7ab7534248bcfea2", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.1.24", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "all flesh like grass", "note": "First Peter quotes the formula that all flesh is like grass and its glory like a grass flower. It is the New Testament's closest verbal and conceptual counterpart to the commentary's juxtaposition of human body and withering pasture."}
{"connection_id": "BC-87ef18586fd6206ce3b8", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.1.25", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "word of the Lord endures", "note": "Peter contrasts withering flesh with the Lord's enduring word, applied to the proclaimed gospel; it develops the commentary's pasture and lasting-life contrast through Christian proclamation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b6d670c1cbab78a37cd7", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.1.3", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "new birth through resurrection to living hope", "note": "First Peter blesses God for giving a new birth into living hope through Jesus' resurrection. It extends the commentary's birth-and-revival nexus into a Christian account of renewed life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-150abd4d2ba88ce7378c", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Thess.4.16", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "command, trumpet, and dead rising", "note": "At the Lord's commanding call, the dead in Christ rise first. It gives a concrete Christian scene of divine sound followed by resurrection, alongside the commentary's recitation and revivification themes."}

