{"connection_id": "BC-5303782034a20e8413f5", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.5.14", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "solid food for the mature", "note": "Hebrews contrasts infants with mature people whose faculties are trained; this develops the commentary's growth-to-maturity sequence as discernment acquired over time."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d9ad4327ce8bb334d9f4", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.1.10", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rich person and fading grass flower", "note": "James tells the rich to boast in lowliness because they pass away like a flower of grass. It closely matches the commentary's confident garden owner and the warning conveyed by worldly life and withering pasture."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2c8d15779801ca907c3d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.1.11", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "sun scorches grass and beauty perishes", "note": "James completes the image: sun scorches the grass, its flower falls, and the rich fade in their pursuits. This gives the image an ethical warning about transient prosperity, as in the commentary's ruined garden."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e276c81588e2800bb221", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.4.14", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "life as a briefly appearing vapor", "note": "James describes present life as vapor that appears briefly and vanishes. It provides a compact ethical analogue to the commentary's warning that worldly life and its flourishing are transient."}
