{"connection_id": "BC-b341673cc3e028eee57d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.1.15", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Spirit from the mother's womb", "note": "John is said to be filled with the Holy Spirit even from his mother's womb. It makes the womb a site of divine life and vocation, a specific New Testament counterpart to the commentary's prenatal life imagery."}
{"connection_id": "BC-98a8164c9efdd298975a", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.1.36", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Elizabeth pregnant in old age", "note": "The angel says Elizabeth, called barren, is now six months pregnant in her old age; this precisely brings the commentary's old-age and approaching-birth senses into one Christian narrative."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e45155e4a31a21660d7f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.1.41", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "infant leaps in Elizabeth's womb", "note": "At Mary's greeting, the infant leaps in Elizabeth's womb; this adds a concrete action of the fetus within the womb to the commentary's prenatal life-stage imagery."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3aa24caad80fb2352f79", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.1.41", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "child leaps in the womb", "note": "At Mary's greeting, the child leaps in Elizabeth's womb. The narrative gives a concrete fetal bodily response to divine visitation, rather than an abstract use of womb language."}
{"connection_id": "BC-54e9cc89989b2d56f225", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.12.20", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "life required from the storing rich man", "note": "God tells the rich planner that his life is demanded that night. The sudden end to stored abundance is a close ethical meeting with the commentary's garden owner who trusts worldly security before it vanishes."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b4a82eafcebfc7949b11", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.2.40", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "child grows strong and wise", "note": "Luke describes Jesus growing as a child, becoming strong and filled with wisdom; it supplies a concrete growth-to-maturity scene for the commentary's Rabb-as-child-rearing branch."}

item.stdout:
{"connection_id": "BC-0932072a97f38a625fe4", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.2.52", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Jesus advances in age and stature", "note": "Jesus increases in wisdom, stature, and favor; this is a direct bodily-growth counterpart to the commentary's reading of maturation through a human life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-df2ae702acf58e6f14df", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.2.52", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "growth in wisdom, stature, and favor", "note": "Jesus advances in wisdom, stature, and favor. It is a concise New Testament life-stage counterpart to the commentary's sense of reaching full youth and ordered maturation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-631a77fdcdf9f7f30a06", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.20.36", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "resurrected people can no longer die", "note": "In Jesus' reply about the resurrection, those deemed worthy of that age can no longer die; this gives a Christian contrast to the surah's condemned who neither die nor live."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3a2a43353099f21bf139", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.4.28", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "crop develops in successive stages", "note": "The earth yields first a stalk, then an ear, then full grain; this plant-growth sequence meets the commentary's child-raised-in-stages and pasture imagery while remaining an agrarian, not human, account."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cb6458abd55763ce2a8d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.4.29", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "harvest when grain is ripe", "note": "The ripe crop is harvested, completing Mark's growth sequence; beside the commentary's vegetation growth and drying, it adds the endpoint of maturity and harvest."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d8321061213ecf0cf32d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.8.36", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "gain the world, forfeit life", "note": "Jesus asks what profit it is to gain the whole world and lose one's life; this states explicitly the danger implicit in the commentary's identification of preferring worldly life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-23fe73e149d526655a94", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.9.48", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Gehenna's worm does not die and fire is not quenched", "note": "Jesus uses Isaiah's undying-worm and unquenched-fire image as a warning about Gehenna; beside the surah's great fire and its continuation that the condemned neither die nor live, it gives a Christian judgment image with a different formulation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4fbd9061db6e4cc8b710", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.20", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "treasure in heaven cannot decay", "note": "Jesus contrasts earthly treasure vulnerable to decay with treasure secured in heaven; this gives a concrete image to the surah's preference for worldly life over what is better and enduring."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d5b5a6ac4731a9a9fe72", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.27", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "no one can add a cubit to stature or lifespan", "note": "Jesus asks whether anxiety can add a cubit to a person's ἡλικία, read as stature or lifespan; this specific ambiguity meets the commentary's measured body and measured human course."}
{"connection_id": "BC-43ccf7d6abb6b54d16ff", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.27", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "unable to add to lifespan", "note": "Jesus asks which anxious person can add a span to life. This is a focused New Testament counterpart to the commentary's measured human lifespan, with a warning against investing trust in present life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-896ff4a96e7a3456f412", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.30", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "field grass clothed today, burned tomorrow", "note": "Jesus says God clothes grass that is thrown into the oven the next day; the image joins the commentary's pasture that withers to bodily care and fire, while Matthew's point is trust rather than judgment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e13b9931a68442faae30", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.30", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "grass today and oven tomorrow", "note": "Jesus describes grass alive today and thrown into an oven tomorrow while arguing for divine care. The image links bodily concern and transient grass, though its immediate point is anxiety rather than resurrection."}
{"connection_id": "BC-879142e2e7b8beee584b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phil.3.21", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Christ transforms the lowly body", "note": "Paul says Christ will transform the body of humiliation to conform to his glorious body; this is a direct Christian counterpart to the commentary's ordered body and resurrection, with transformation rather than mere return."}
{"connection_id": "BC-eefdef7b3ff22991c70c", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phil.3.21", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "transformation of the lowly body", "note": "Christ will transform the body of lowliness to conform to his glorious body. The verse gives a specifically Christian answer to the commentary's movement from mortal formed body to life after death."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f185d03fe6d757cdf1ac", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.9.6", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "people seek death, but death flees", "note": "During the locust torment, people desire death without finding it; this concretely meets the surah's adjacent statement that those in the fire neither die nor live, although Revelation's torment scene is not itself the final lake of fire."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3976ed22785ffe81464b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.4.17", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "gives life to dead and calls what is not", "note": "Paul characterizes God as giving life to the dead and calling into being things that are not. The paired creation-and-revival formula closely matches the commentary's use of fetal creation as evidence for resurrection."}
{"connection_id": "BC-80af4533beae551c518e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.8.11", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Spirit gives life to mortal bodies", "note": "The Spirit who raised Jesus will give life to mortal bodies. It directly joins divine indwelling spirit, bodily mortality, and resurrection in the way the commentary connects formation, breath, and revivification."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e98ee1452e482819b8dc", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.8.22", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "creation groans in childbirth", "note": "Paul pictures all creation groaning in labor pains; this brings the commentary's womb-centered bodily life cycle into a cosmic creation image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d4b3af2d259eb4992a94", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.8.22", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "creation groans in labor pains", "note": "Paul figures the whole creation as groaning together in childbirth. The verse applies labor imagery to creation's movement toward redemption, an interpretive extension of the commentary's body, earth, and resurrection linkage."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5cb81e37791202c98d44", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.8.23", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "redemption of the body awaited", "note": "Believers groan while awaiting bodily redemption; this connects the commentary's formed human body and resurrection argument with Paul's future transformation of the body."}
{"connection_id": "BC-78b48b03a6726ba1c1d8", "ref": "2 Maccabees 7:11", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "martyr expects to receive his limbs again", "note": "A tortured brother says he received his limbs from heaven and hopes to receive them again; this Jewish witness makes resurrection bodily and specific, beside the commentary's inference from bodily creation to raising the dead."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5eeae764af22977da0d2", "ref": "2 Maccabees 7:14", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "king has no resurrection to life", "note": "The youngest brother expects God to raise him to life, while the king has no resurrection to life; this adds the commentary's bodily revival theme together with opposed postmortem destinies."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1635d143e0934d5acf96", "ref": "2 Maccabees 7:22", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "mother cannot explain womb formation", "note": "The mother of the martyrs says she does not know how her children came to be in her womb and denies that she gave them life or set their elements in order. It closely mirrors the commentary's created, ordered fetus while locating agency in the Creator."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cc3bfd5403219aa6f8ac", "ref": "2 Maccabees 7:23", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Creator returns life and breath", "note": "The mother says the Creator will return life and breath to her sons. The verse directly makes prenatal formation an argument for resurrection, exactly the inferential move foregrounded by the commentary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dfb38e1e37896fdaa757", "ref": "2 Maccabees 7:28", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "creation and human coming-to-be", "note": "The mother points to heaven, earth, and the human race as made by God. In her martyrdom context this supports hope beyond death, extending the commentary's creation-to-resurrection reasoning."}

