{"connection_id": "BC-2b664d83f0ad181df2a0", "ref": "4 Ezra 3:5", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "dust-made body and breath of life", "note": "Fourth Ezra says God commanded dust to give Adam, a lifeless body formed by divine hands, then breathed life into him. The retelling explicitly combines the commentary's earth, fashioned body, and breath sequence."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e4c9ee883970428e9848", "ref": "Berakhot 60b", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "formed in wisdom; openings and cavities", "note": "The Asher Yatzar blessing praises the one who formed the human in wisdom with openings and cavities whose proper functioning sustains life. It offers a Jewish liturgical analogue to the commentary's well-proportioned, disease-free body ordered by wisdom."}
{"connection_id": "BC-acba409cfd433d8df30f", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 3:1", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "origin in a drop; return to dust", "note": "Avot asks a person to remember the bodily origin in a drop and the destination of dust and decay. Its ethical memento turns the life-course images of the commentary into a warning against worldly self-assurance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-55d4f5d2b36194c3e5d8", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 4:22 (numbering varies)", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "formed, born, lives, dies, and gives account", "note": "Avot presents human formation, birth, life, death, and judgment as an involuntary ordered sequence. It is a rabbinic whole-life formulation for the stages the commentary assembles from womb to old age and final reckoning."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0addff8166363607a74c", "ref": "Niddah 30b", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "fetus taught Torah, then made to forget", "note": "The Talmud says an angel teaches the fetus Torah in the womb and strikes it at birth so it forgets; this meets the commentary's womb association for the root of 87:6 with that verse's recitation and not-forgetting, while the Talmud's fetus and Qur'anic addressee differ."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0bdc9e519298cb307c6e", "ref": "Niddah 30b", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "fetus in womb taught Torah then made to forget", "note": "Niddah says the fetus is taught Torah in the womb and an angel causes it to forget at birth. The combination of womb, instruction, and forgetting is a specific meeting with Q 87:6's recitation and not-forgetting beside the commentary's womb sense for its root."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7c2229902bbceaa3450f", "ref": "Niddah 31a", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "parents' material and God's life-giving share", "note": "The Talmud distinguishes parental contributions to bones, flesh, and other features from God's gift of spirit, soul, senses, and understanding. It is a detailed Jewish account of an ordered body joined to life and capacity."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d304c7f44c4bcfcd44a9", "ref": "Sanhedrin 90b", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "buried grain emerges clothed", "note": "Rabbi Meir answers a question about resurrection by pointing to grain buried bare that emerges clothed; this Jewish argument uses plant transformation to make renewed embodiment imaginable, meeting the commentary's pairing of bodily creation, resurrection, and vegetation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8f38ae3571c16834d337", "ref": "Sanhedrin 90b", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "grain buried and clothed in rising", "note": "Rabbi Meir answers a resurrection question with wheat that is buried bare and emerges clothed. The rabbinic analogy joins vegetal renewal to bodily resurrection, like the commentary's body-and-pasture pairing."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a662ed9d23f739104d7f", "ref": "Sirach 16:26", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "creatures assigned their order", "note": "Sirach describes the Lord assigning created works their portions and arranging them in order. It is a Jewish development of the commentary's claim that each thing is put in a particular measure and mode."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ba210cbe88fc7a2f8b1a", "ref": "Sirach 17:1", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "human from earth and returned to it", "note": "Sirach says the Lord created humans from earth and lets them return to it. It adds a sapiential version of the soil-to-body-to-death arc underlying the commentary's resurrection reflection."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4af9769ce7f0570e3e5b", "ref": "Sirach 17:6", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "tongue, eyes, ears, mind, understanding", "note": "Sirach attributes human tongue, eyes, ears, mind, knowledge, and understanding to God. It offers a sapiential expansion of the commentary's properly ordered body into its sensory and cognitive capacities."}
{"connection_id": "BC-573849849d7559780d3f", "ref": "Sirach 40:1", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "from leaving the womb to returning to the mother of all living", "note": "Sirach describes human toil as spanning birth from the womb to return to earth, an unusually explicit lifespan parallel to the commentary's womb-to-aging sequence."}
{"connection_id": "BC-72865b44806c2ee0eabd", "ref": "Sirach 40:1", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "from mother's womb to return to earth", "note": "Sirach frames the human burden from leaving the mother's womb until returning to the mother of all. It explicitly spans the bodily life course from birth to death that the commentary assembles from its roots."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3406a140aaf85d636668", "ref": "Targum Onkelos on Genesis 2:7", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "living being rendered speaking spirit", "note": "Onkelos renders the human made living in Genesis as a speaking spirit. That interpretive rendering links the formed and breathed body to speech, which helps place Q 87:6's recitation beside the commentary's bodily reading of its root."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4c6c906632e2ed1d7665", "ref": "Targum Onqelos to Genesis 2:7", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "living soul rendered as speaking spirit", "note": "Onqelos interprets the formed human's life-breath as a speaking spirit; this Jewish reading connects the bodily formation discussed for 87:2 with the surah's adjacent gift of recitation in 87:6."}
{"connection_id": "BC-11dce505d1842dcdfd5f", "ref": "WLC:Dan.12.2", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "sleepers in dust awake to opposed destinies", "note": "Daniel says many sleeping in the dust awake to everlasting life or shame; this gives a Hebrew Bible resurrection counterpart to the commentary's creation-to-resurrection argument and places judgment after bodily renewal."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c39f9e938e70721c8a28", "ref": "WLC:Dan.12.2", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "sleepers in dust awaken", "note": "Daniel depicts many sleeping in earth's dust awakening to divergent lasting destinies. It gives the eschatological endpoint of the commentary's earth-to-body-to-resurrection arc."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2a5e851da00dd3bc5128", "ref": "WLC:Deut.32.18", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Israel forgets the God who gave it birth", "note": "Moses accuses Israel of forgetting the God who gave it birth; this meets the commentary's womb and child-raising senses beside 87:6's promise not to forget, but reverses the roles: Israel forgets its divine parent."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9e156ba9b7e50d0c64fc", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.11.5", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "bones formed in a pregnant woman's womb", "note": "Ecclesiastes contrasts the unknown formation of bones in the womb with God's work as maker of all; this directly joins the commentary's fetal body-shaping and divine ordering, while stressing that the process is hidden."}
{"connection_id": "BC-06e139cb39eae82f4620", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.11.5", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "bones in a pregnant womb", "note": "Ecclesiastes invokes the formation of bones in a pregnant woman as something humans do not know, then extends that ignorance to God's work. It checks any attempt to turn the commentary's lexical fetal images into a claimed account of fetal mechanics."}
{"connection_id": "BC-37f477700ed374475d76", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.12.1", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "remember the Creator before aging days", "note": "Ecclesiastes urges remembrance of the Creator before difficult years and old age arrive, joining the commentary's Creator-centered bodily life course to its age-related reading."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f4713dde40d8cdee1599", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.12.1", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "remember Creator in days of youth", "note": "Ecclesiastes calls for remembering the Creator during youth before the difficult days arrive. It makes youth and later decline an ethical frame, matching the commentary's use of completed youth and old age as life stages."}
{"connection_id": "BC-279ec0ae7281060e9be2", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.12.3", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "old-age bodily decline", "note": "The poem pictures trembling hands, bent strength, failing teeth, and dim eyes; it gives physical detail to the commentary's otherwise brief old-age sense."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a476545b2b38c37fc3db", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.12.5", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "fear of heights and journey to the eternal home", "note": "The old-age poem moves from bodily frailty to the human going to an eternal home, adding the life's endpoint to the commentary's aging stage."}

