{"connection_id": "BC-c39f9e938e70721c8a28", "ref": "WLC:Dan.12.2", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "sleepers in dust awaken", "note": "Daniel depicts many sleeping in earth's dust awakening to divergent lasting destinies. It gives the eschatological endpoint of the commentary's earth-to-body-to-resurrection arc."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2a5e851da00dd3bc5128", "ref": "WLC:Deut.32.18", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Israel forgets the God who gave it birth", "note": "Moses accuses Israel of forgetting the God who gave it birth; this meets the commentary's womb and child-raising senses beside 87:6's promise not to forget, but reverses the roles: Israel forgets its divine parent."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9e156ba9b7e50d0c64fc", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.11.5", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "bones formed in a pregnant woman's womb", "note": "Ecclesiastes contrasts the unknown formation of bones in the womb with God's work as maker of all; this directly joins the commentary's fetal body-shaping and divine ordering, while stressing that the process is hidden."}
{"connection_id": "BC-06e139cb39eae82f4620", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.11.5", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "bones in a pregnant womb", "note": "Ecclesiastes invokes the formation of bones in a pregnant woman as something humans do not know, then extends that ignorance to God's work. It checks any attempt to turn the commentary's lexical fetal images into a claimed account of fetal mechanics."}
{"connection_id": "BC-37f477700ed374475d76", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.12.1", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "remember the Creator before aging days", "note": "Ecclesiastes urges remembrance of the Creator before difficult years and old age arrive, joining the commentary's Creator-centered bodily life course to its age-related reading."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f4713dde40d8cdee1599", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.12.1", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "remember Creator in days of youth", "note": "Ecclesiastes calls for remembering the Creator during youth before the difficult days arrive. It makes youth and later decline an ethical frame, matching the commentary's use of completed youth and old age as life stages."}
{"connection_id": "BC-279ec0ae7281060e9be2", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.12.3", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "old-age bodily decline", "note": "The poem pictures trembling hands, bent strength, failing teeth, and dim eyes; it gives physical detail to the commentary's otherwise brief old-age sense."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a476545b2b38c37fc3db", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.12.5", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "fear of heights and journey to the eternal home", "note": "The old-age poem moves from bodily frailty to the human going to an eternal home, adding the life's endpoint to the commentary's aging stage."}

item.stdout:
{"connection_id": "BC-d8c531c57ac9bcef6211", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.12.7", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "dust returns and spirit returns", "note": "At death, dust returns to earth and spirit to God; this reverses the dust-and-breath formation of Genesis 2:7 and supplies an endpoint to the commentary's bodily lifespan, without itself arguing for resurrection."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4e80c153e0bc9b414885", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.12.7", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "dust returns; spirit returns to God", "note": "Ecclesiastes separates the body's return to earth from the spirit's return to God. It gives a sharply stated deathward counterpart to the commentary's body-and-breath account and its afterlife horizon."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d428ae02dbba56d51e5e", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.3.2", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "time to be born and time to die", "note": "Ecclesiastes frames human life with a time for birth and a time for death, a concise lifespan formula beside the commentary's womb-to-old-age reading."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9676caecd627a0c47d34", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.16.4", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "newborn with cord uncut, unwashed, and unswaddled", "note": "The commentary's camel idiom mentions fetal membranes not gathered; Ezekiel's allegory depicts a newborn left with its cord uncut and without birth care. The objects differ, but both make perinatal handling concrete."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f847e816a572bfb05ba4", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.37.10", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the breath enters and the slain live", "note": "The bodies stand alive after the breath enters them; this is a vivid resurrection-like image beside the commentary's argument from bodily formation to God's power to raise the dead, though Ezekiel interprets the vision corporately."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7b7637ea70d28759ceb8", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.37.10", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "breath gives life to assembled bodies", "note": "Once breath enters, the assembled bodies live and stand. The verse completes the formation-to-life sequence that the commentary traces from fetal body to resurrection."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d46176de6b7804e5c8b5", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.37.14", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "spirit placed within; life restored", "note": "God promises to put his spirit within the people so that they live. Its own national-restoration setting makes the bodily image an interpretive rather than a simple individual-resurrection equation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-584efce8b77fa69d2e95", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.37.5", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "breath enters dry bones", "note": "In Ezekiel's vision God promises breath to dry bones so that they will live. It supplies an explicit bodily-revival analogue for the commentary's citation of embryonic creation as evidence for raising the dead."}
{"connection_id": "BC-653c76e5001347c59f75", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.37.6", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "sinews, flesh, skin, and breath restore a body", "note": "In a vision interpreted as Israel's restoration (37:11), God reassembles the dry bones with bodily tissues and breath; this supplies a detailed scriptural image of the ordered body and renewed life discussed in the commentary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8f9bba76e6862a7fe6f0", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.37.6", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "sinews, flesh, skin, then breath", "note": "God orders sinews, flesh, skin, and breath onto bones. The ordered rebuilding of a body is a close counterpart to formed flesh, bodily proportion, and later life; Ezekiel's own vision then identifies this with Israel's restoration."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8413c0cd61a3b06bd1eb", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.37.8", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "body assembled but still without breath", "note": "The bones have sinews, flesh, and skin, yet no breath; this makes bodily formation and animation distinct stages, sharpening the commentary's creation-and-order sequence."}
{"connection_id": "BC-24b2d7983784a46aad85", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.37.9", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "breath summoned from the four winds to the slain", "note": "The prophet is told to call breath into the slain so they may live; this joins the commentary's formed body with divine breath and revival, in a vision whose stated referent is exiled Israel."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2ff13c2eef199f191a15", "ref": "WLC:Gen.1.28", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Hebrew rabah, multiplying human life", "note": "Hebrew rabah, to become many, is a cognate family relevant to the commentary's source label r-b-b. Genesis uses it for human fruitfulness and multiplication, not for the Arabic sense of rearing a child stage by stage."}
{"connection_id": "BC-863753f1f1a2ff40db41", "ref": "WLC:Gen.18.11", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Sarah and Abraham advanced in age; her monthly cycle had ceased", "note": "The narrative names the aged body's reproductive limit before Isaac's promised birth, bringing the commentary's old-age and approaching-birth stages together in one concrete case."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9db70b0e9b6fe5ea7ab5", "ref": "WLC:Gen.2.19", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Hebrew qara, calling names after formation", "note": "Hebrew qara, to call or name, belongs to the Semitic q-r-ʾ family also heard in Q 87:6's recitation verb. Here Adam names creatures after their formation, so it supports speech after creation but does not carry the commentary's Arabic womb-closure sense."}
{"connection_id": "BC-71bd4e8d6e385a95fe85", "ref": "WLC:Gen.2.7", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "formed earthling and life-breath", "note": "The commentary reads 87:2's creating and proportioning bodily; Genesis narrates God forming the first human from ground and breathing life into him, adding the dust-to-living-body sequence."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6b1f87867f6aca843dbe", "ref": "WLC:Gen.2.7", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "dust formed; breath of life", "note": "Genesis forms the human from ground and then gives life by divine breath. It provides a close biblical sequence for the commentary's ordered body and its cited Q 32:9 breath, while retaining an earthly material origin."}
{"connection_id": "BC-137cda15f9bbd77be93d", "ref": "WLC:Gen.21.2", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Sarah conceives and bears Isaac in old age", "note": "Isaac is born to Sarah at the time God promised, when Abraham is old; this gives a narrative reversal in which the commentary's late-life stage becomes the setting for birth."}
{"connection_id": "BC-80ed62c3b4c21791eebe", "ref": "WLC:Gen.21.7", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Sarah nurses a son in Abraham's old age", "note": "Sarah marvels that she has borne and nursed a son in Abraham's old age; this uniquely combines the commentary's child-rearing and senescence stages."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9aa4159e938a5cc3da4c", "ref": "WLC:Gen.3.19", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "formed-from-dust human returns to dust", "note": "After the human is taken from the ground, God says he will return to it; this completes the earth-to-body sequence cited in the commentary with its bodily endpoint before the Qur'anic resurrection argument."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0de3569b58e95c427d57", "ref": "WLC:Gen.3.19", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "return to dust", "note": "The human formed from ground is told to return to dust. It supplies the deathward side of the commentary's earth, body, and resurrection sequence, though it does not itself promise revival."}
{"connection_id": "BC-facdefd445b47626cce0", "ref": "WLC:Hos.11.3", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "taught Ephraim to walk and lifted him by the arms", "note": "God recalls teaching Israel as a child to walk and carrying him; this is a concrete child-rearing scene for the commentary's Rabb-as-raising-a-child sense."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2d57495681058c55abcc", "ref": "WLC:Hos.11.4", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "lifted infant and bent down to feed", "note": "God describes lifting Israel like an infant and bending down to feed him; this extends the commentary's nurturing life-stage image into hands-on parental care."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6cfb05b2b1f8f803d917", "ref": "WLC:Isa.26.19", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "dead awaken and earth gives birth to shades", "note": "Isaiah calls the dead to live and the earth to give birth to its dead; this joins bodily resurrection to birth imagery, meeting the commentary's fetal formation and resurrection argument."}
{"connection_id": "BC-97468f9a81ae1aa0e307", "ref": "WLC:Isa.26.19", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "earth gives birth to the dead", "note": "Isaiah calls sleepers in dust to awake and portrays earth as giving birth to the dead. The birth image makes an especially close counterpart to the commentary's inference from prenatal formation to resurrection."}

