{"connection_id": "BC-880947364cf578ec88db", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.6", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "all flesh is grass", "note": "Isaiah identifies all flesh with grass and its glory with a field flower; this makes the commentary's pairing of human body and drying pasture an explicit image of mortality."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e93566115eba05828a0e", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.6", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "all flesh is grass", "note": "Isaiah's voice calls all flesh grass and human constancy a field flower. The explicit flesh-and-grass equation strongly parallels the commentary's body and drying pasture as two faces of one warning."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bab28857a9db23d9189c", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.7", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "breath and withering grass", "note": "Grass withers and the flower fades when the Lord's breath blows on it. It adds the agency of divine breath to the commentary's life-and-pasture contrast."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e8339e1642510060cf65", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.8", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "grass fades, God's word endures", "note": "Isaiah contrasts withering grass and fading flowers with God's enduring word; beside the surah's dried pasture and its enduring-afterlife contrast, it adds a biblical word-versus-mortal-life formulation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b121ff82f16525b11400", "ref": "WLC:Isa.42.5", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "breath to people and spirit to walkers", "note": "Isaiah's creator gives breath to the people on earth and spirit to those who walk on it. This is a compact creation-and-life formulation alongside the commentary's Q 32:9 comparison."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3f4d9300deb5306f7fd1", "ref": "WLC:Isa.44.2", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "maker and former in womb", "note": "Isaiah names the Lord as both maker and one who forms the addressee from the womb. This directly joins the two creation verbs that the commentary develops as fetal formation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-10b2ab6380ed2ab24b70", "ref": "WLC:Isa.44.24", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Creator says he formed Israel from the womb", "note": "Isaiah joins forming the addressee in the womb to the Lord's making of all things; this ties the commentary's prenatal formation directly to universal creation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7d836d5ac7b3fd055959", "ref": "WLC:Isa.44.24", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "former from the womb", "note": "The speaker again identifies himself as maker of all and former of the addressee from the womb. Its cosmic and prenatal scopes together illuminate the commentary's claim that a measured human body belongs to a larger created order."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6eead0bdf572567368bb", "ref": "WLC:Isa.46.3", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "borne from the womb", "note": "God says he has borne and carried Israel from the womb; this makes the commentary's Rabb-as-raising image a lifelong divine-care metaphor, though Isaiah speaks collectively of Israel."}
{"connection_id": "BC-106c60f63a5dfdb436a3", "ref": "WLC:Isa.46.3", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "borne from belly and womb", "note": "God addresses those carried from the belly and borne from the womb. It begins a divine care sequence at prenatal and birth stages, matching the commentary's life-course reading."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0cf7bc36988909823744", "ref": "WLC:Isa.46.4", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "carried to gray hairs", "note": "The same oracle promises to carry Israel into old age and gray hairs, bringing the commentary's womb-to-senescence life arc under one image of divine care."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9fee8776c446bea1b6d8", "ref": "WLC:Isa.46.4", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "until old age and gray hair", "note": "The same divine bearer remains present until old age and gray hair. This is a particularly exact lifespan counterpart to the commentary's movement from the womb through maturity to senescence."}
{"connection_id": "BC-42ec7832872617a8bbd6", "ref": "WLC:Isa.49.15", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "mother, nursing child, and memory", "note": "Isaiah asks whether a mother can forget the son of her womb, then says God will not forget Zion; this meets the commentary's womb, nurture, and not-forgetting cluster through a maternal-memory image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ac98b7866b27406c4c7c", "ref": "WLC:Isa.49.5", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "formed from womb as servant", "note": "The servant says the Lord formed him from the womb for service. It joins bodily formation to calling, a useful counterpart both to Q 87:2's formation and to the recited commission in Q 87:6."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1698bb5e5b3ead74b6db", "ref": "WLC:Isa.51.12", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "mortal human made like grass", "note": "Isaiah calls a mortal human being like grass. This directly joins the human body and fading vegetation that the commentary treats as one warning about temporary worldly life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5622e601b83a1c170815", "ref": "WLC:Isa.57.16", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "spirit and breath made by God", "note": "Isaiah says that the spirit would fail before God, along with the breaths God has made. The verse joins creaturely frailty with divine authorship of breath, though its immediate context is divine restraint from wrath."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b502053a24e99f58df3f", "ref": "WLC:Isa.66.24", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "corpses, undying worm, unquenched fire", "note": "Isaiah's closing vision has worshipers view the corpses of rebels with undying worms and unquenched fire; beside 87:12–13, it shares fire and undying imagery but depicts corpses rather than living people unable to die."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4ffa8cd389693eb668b7", "ref": "WLC:Jer.1.5", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "formed in womb before prophetic commission", "note": "Jeremiah is known and set apart before being formed in the womb; this shares the commentary's divine prenatal formation while adding vocation before birth."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b49f6f2da948e7c107ad", "ref": "WLC:Jer.1.5", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "formed, known, and appointed before birth", "note": "Jeremiah is known before womb-formation and appointed before emergence. The verse makes divine formation part of a life directed toward a purpose, sharpening the commentary's measure-and-guidance claim."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c9a085dcee811a1b36b0", "ref": "WLC:Job.1.21", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "from mother's womb to naked return", "note": "Job says he came naked from his mother's womb and will return naked. The saying compresses bodily origin and mortality into the life-course arc that the commentary traces from womb formation to its end."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c0896f1a96ec8efbbfef", "ref": "WLC:Job.10.10", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "milk poured and curdled", "note": "Job pictures his own formation in the womb as milk becoming cheese; this gives concrete gestational process to the commentary's fetal-forming life-stage reading."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a2a58855291b69b59dfc", "ref": "WLC:Job.10.10", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "milk poured and curdled", "note": "Job figures his prenatal making as liquid poured like milk and curdled like cheese. That material, staged image belongs beside the commentary's formed and unformed embryonic body."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ba71f8356fa1e9ecadac", "ref": "WLC:Job.10.11", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "skin, flesh, bones, sinews", "note": "Job says God clothed and knit these body parts together, closely specifying the bodily ordering that the commentary hears in 87:2–3."}
{"connection_id": "BC-670d7390246e8af9032d", "ref": "WLC:Job.10.11", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "skin, flesh, bones, sinews", "note": "Job describes being clothed with skin and flesh and woven with bones and sinews. It is a concrete bodily counterpart to the commentary's claim that creation and setting right can be heard as bodily formation."}
