{"connection_id": "BC-c0896f1a96ec8efbbfef", "ref": "WLC:Job.10.10", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "milk poured and curdled", "note": "Job pictures his own formation in the womb as milk becoming cheese; this gives concrete gestational process to the commentary's fetal-forming life-stage reading."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a2a58855291b69b59dfc", "ref": "WLC:Job.10.10", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "milk poured and curdled", "note": "Job figures his prenatal making as liquid poured like milk and curdled like cheese. That material, staged image belongs beside the commentary's formed and unformed embryonic body."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ba71f8356fa1e9ecadac", "ref": "WLC:Job.10.11", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "skin, flesh, bones, sinews", "note": "Job says God clothed and knit these body parts together, closely specifying the bodily ordering that the commentary hears in 87:2–3."}
{"connection_id": "BC-670d7390246e8af9032d", "ref": "WLC:Job.10.11", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "skin, flesh, bones, sinews", "note": "Job describes being clothed with skin and flesh and woven with bones and sinews. It is a concrete bodily counterpart to the commentary's claim that creation and setting right can be heard as bodily formation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0ea9e945b102fd84f2b0", "ref": "WLC:Job.10.12", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "life granted and spirit guarded", "note": "After the anatomical images, Job says God granted life and guarded his spirit. It joins formed flesh to a divinely sustained life, as the commentary joins proportioning to breath and eventual revivification."}

item.stdout:
{"connection_id": "BC-0ffcf6e97e102dda51fe", "ref": "WLC:Job.10.8", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God's hands fashion and make", "note": "Job addresses God as the maker who fashioned and made him, a personal bodily counterpart to the commentary's account of creation and arrangement."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4576653c8675d41e5a35", "ref": "WLC:Job.10.8", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "maker's hands and destruction", "note": "Job says God's hands fashioned him all around, then protests that the same maker turns to destroy him. The shared embodied formation is set in a lament, a deliberate tension with the praise of creating and proportioning in Q 87:2."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0b31a6cbe5085920187d", "ref": "WLC:Job.14.1", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "human born of woman, brief as a fading flower", "note": "Job joins birth from a woman to a short life that flowers and withers; this connects the commentary's lifespan arc to its body-and-drying-vegetation warning."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e0eb7ec5823744afbc12", "ref": "WLC:Job.14.14", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Job asks whether a dead person will live again", "note": "Job asks whether a human who dies will live again and says he would wait for release; this places the commentary's confident move from bodily formation to resurrection beside a Hebrew lament that frames renewed life as a question and hope."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dbd43cd0deccdb3f13ac", "ref": "WLC:Job.14.5", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "days numbered and lifespan bounded", "note": "Job says a person's days are determined, months numbered, and limit set; this closely meets the commentary's reading of 87:3 as assigning each thing its measure, here applied to human lifespan."}
{"connection_id": "BC-af832d99e0b5cbed922c", "ref": "WLC:Job.14.5", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "determined days and bounds", "note": "Job says a person's days and months are determined and bounded by God. This directly supplies the temporal side of the commentary's particular measure (qaddara) across a human life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-42adf22c8638137afa90", "ref": "WLC:Job.31.15", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "one maker fashioned both people in the womb", "note": "Job grounds equality between himself and his servant in the same maker forming them in the womb; this adds an ethical consequence to the commentary's account of shared human bodily formation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7a97c5cc0462ddf62adb", "ref": "WLC:Job.31.15", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "made and fashioned in womb", "note": "Job grounds equal human standing in one maker who made both people in the womb and fashioned them there. The shared point is divine womb-formation, not merely a general creator title."}
{"connection_id": "BC-097b75aef7d892720922", "ref": "WLC:Job.33.4", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Spirit made me; breath gives life", "note": "Job's interlocutor says God's Spirit made him and the Almighty's breath gives him life. It is a particularly direct Hebrew Bible counterpart to the commentary's formed body followed by enlivening breath."}
{"connection_id": "BC-326a1502f6fa080dda8e", "ref": "WLC:Job.34.14", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "spirit and breath gathered back", "note": "Elihu imagines God gathering back to himself a creature's spirit and breath. This supplies the withdrawal side of the commentary's body-and-breath sequence."}
{"connection_id": "BC-031382a999fe4d3826f8", "ref": "WLC:Job.34.15", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "all flesh perishes and returns to dust", "note": "The consequence of withdrawn breath is that all flesh dies and humanity returns to dust. It makes the body, breath, and earth sequence explicit before any resurrection answer is supplied."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4180a3a04c226ddc447c", "ref": "WLC:Job.36.5", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Hebrew kabbir, great or mighty", "note": "Job calls God kabbir, great or mighty, a cognate family of Arabic kabir. The Hebrew context keeps greatness rather than age in view, which helps distinguish the commentary's age-related Arabic root note from the actual 'great' fire of Q 87:12."}
{"connection_id": "BC-234cced6fe1109a6053e", "ref": "WLC:Prov.20.29", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "youthful strength and gray-haired age", "note": "Proverbs contrasts young men's strength with gray hair as elders' splendor, giving a compact scriptural life-stage pair beside the commentary's maturity and senescence readings."}
{"connection_id": "BC-89dd7d19c060b715b281", "ref": "WLC:Ps.103.14", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "God knows the frame and remembers dust", "note": "The psalm says God knows the human frame and remembers that humans are dust. It ties bodily construction to earthly frailty, supplementing the commentary's formed body and its eventual decline."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2e6a00afaa643d7d3830", "ref": "WLC:Ps.103.15", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "human days like grass and field flower", "note": "The psalm compares human days and flourishing to grass and a flower, directly joining bodily lifespan to vegetation as the commentary does in its life-stage and pasture reading."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5e6300957b87b75d8d2e", "ref": "WLC:Ps.103.15", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "human days as grass", "note": "The psalm explicitly compares a human's days to grass and a flowering field. It closely matches the commentary's pairing of the ordered body with the pasture that dries up as one warning about mortal life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f4d4574b5a3b6f2eaba0", "ref": "WLC:Ps.103.16", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wind erases the flower's place", "note": "The flower disappears when wind passes; this adds the disappearance of mortal life to the commentary's warning carried by drying pasture."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9d00683a1341c41a2d20", "ref": "WLC:Ps.103.16", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wind passes; grass gone", "note": "The following verse makes the grass vanish when the wind passes over it. It supplies the disappearance that gives the body-and-pasture comparison its warning force."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7d5ebc21781c026a4d77", "ref": "WLC:Ps.104.29", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "breath withdrawn; dust returned", "note": "When God takes away breath, living creatures die and return to dust. This links breath, bodily mortality, and earth in the same sequence the commentary connects to creation and resurrection."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a67a209437ce15d3756b", "ref": "WLC:Ps.104.30", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "breath sent; creatures created", "note": "God sends breath, creatures are created, and the ground is renewed. The verse places animate creation and revived earth together, paralleling the commentary's juxtaposition of body and watered pasture."}
{"connection_id": "BC-02e55b2b70477ee2e352", "ref": "WLC:Ps.119.73", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "hands made and fashioned; request for understanding", "note": "The psalmist says divine hands made and fashioned him, then asks for understanding of commandments. It brings formed person and divinely directed understanding together, bridging Q 87:2-3's creation and guidance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9fcd095a5a0e5fa90a9f", "ref": "WLC:Ps.139.13", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "knit together in the womb", "note": "The psalmist credits God with forming inward parts and knitting him in his mother's womb, making explicit the prenatal bodily scene behind the commentary's life-stage reading."}
{"connection_id": "BC-db7817319a5ab47a5998", "ref": "WLC:Ps.139.13", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "knit in mother's womb", "note": "The psalm attributes the inward parts and covering in the mother's womb to God. It is a direct counterpart to the commentary's womb enclosure and fetal bodily formation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fe239cfedeba7f6d12e8", "ref": "WLC:Ps.139.15", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "frame secretly woven", "note": "The psalm's hidden, intricately woven formation in the body's depths adds a concealed-making image to the commentary's womb and shaping sequence."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5a23be5ec6080cddb16b", "ref": "WLC:Ps.139.15", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "frame made secretly and woven", "note": "The speaker's frame is made in secret and woven in hidden depths. Its hidden craftsmanship gives a biblical form to the commentary's transition from unformed to fully shaped body."}

