{"connection_id": "BC-9643fad6e4be4b26336e", "ref": "WLC:Ps.139.16", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "unformed substance and fashioned days", "note": "God sees the speaker's unformed substance, and the days are written before they arrive. The verse combines embryo imagery with a measured lifespan, the two strands of the commentary's reading of created and apportioned life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6dc8633d4b8a7dea9f51", "ref": "WLC:Ps.39.4", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "measure of days and life's end", "note": "The psalm asks God to make known the measure of its days and its end; this makes the commentary's measured-life reading of 87:3 a prayer about human limits."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a2cd75051fb046f21fd8", "ref": "WLC:Ps.39.4", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "measure and number of days", "note": "The psalmist asks to know the measure and extent of his days. This gives a personal, prayerful analogue to the commentary's understanding of qaddara as a life set to a measure."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d71e0734b8c29ba0b0d0", "ref": "WLC:Ps.71.6", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God relied on from the womb", "note": "The speaker says God has been his hope from birth and benefactor from the womb, an individual prayer counterpart to the commentary's nurturing life-cycle sense of Rabb."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ac5e3d8a959a89b78049", "ref": "WLC:Ps.71.6", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "upheld from the womb", "note": "The psalmist places dependence on God from the mother's belly onward. Together with its old-age plea, it gives the lifespan arc that the commentary hears in child-rearing and senescence."}
