{"connection_id": "BC-e07c274266a4ed077ef4", "ref": "WLC:Ps.71.9", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "old age and failing strength", "note": "The same speaker asks not to be abandoned in old age when strength fails. This is a specific biblical counterpart to the commentary's move from fetal formation through maturity toward old age."}
{"connection_id": "BC-49c362e572c77b533d50", "ref": "WLC:Ps.90.10", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "measured years swiftly pass", "note": "The psalm assigns human life seventy or, at strength, eighty years, then says it passes swiftly; this makes the commentary's measured-body and old-age senses an explicit lifespan measure."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b5a59d7f128300b1bee1", "ref": "WLC:Ps.90.12", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "number our days for wisdom", "note": "The psalm asks God to teach people to number their days; it joins the commentary's measured lifespan to the guidance of 87:3, with numbering leading to wise living."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2b57882cc883c66127c1", "ref": "WLC:Ps.92.14", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "fruitful and green in old age", "note": "The righteous remain fruitful, fresh, and green in old age; the verse uniquely joins the commentary's aging body with its surah's pasture and vegetation imagery."}
{"connection_id": "BC-290a4893b9c3adbbc9b4", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 11:20", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "all things ordered by measure, number, and weight", "note": "Wisdom says God ordered all things by measure, number, and weight; this closely specifies the commentary's reading of 87:3 as putting each thing in its particular measure and form."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b46dbf7fa6134eff4d4a", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 11:20", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "measure, number, and weight", "note": "Wisdom says God arranged all things by measure, number, and weight. This is an unusually exact Jewish formulation of the commentary's account of qaddara as placing each thing in a particular measure and form."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5dce735af4adbbe125b6", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 16:13", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "power over life, death, and return", "note": "Wisdom attributes authority over life and death to God, including bringing people down to Hades and back. It is a direct Jewish formulation of the creation-to-resurrection inference developed in the commentary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e987f8a101933c3b1c50", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 2:23", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "created for incorruption and eternity", "note": "Wisdom says God created humanity for incorruption and made it in the likeness of divine eternity. It gives a Jewish counterweight to bodily decay that closely matches the commentary's contrast of worldly life with what endures."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ae0ee1bcd5740ba8d6be", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 7:1", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "mortal, earth-formed human", "note": "Solomon presents himself as mortal and descended from the first human formed from earth; this places the commentary's bodily ordering within a Jewish reflection on universal human origin."}
{"connection_id": "BC-56d19dd1c5364393cc88", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 7:1", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "molded into flesh in mother's womb", "note": "Wisdom's speaker says he was molded into flesh in a mother's womb. It is a late Jewish, explicitly prenatal formulation of the bodily shaping developed in the commentary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-238ddcaf4901c5fbffa7", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 7:2", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "flesh molded in the womb", "note": "Solomon describes being molded in flesh in his mother's womb over ten months, a direct gestational counterpart to the commentary's fetal formation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ee41d6cc5c4e35fb9f7c", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 7:2", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "ten months, blood, and seed", "note": "The next verse describes gestation in a fixed period and bodily material from seed and blood. Its timed, material account closely meets the commentary's womb gathering and measured formation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-208d657b0c338c87701e", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 7:3", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "first breath and birth cry", "note": "The infant receives breath and cries at birth; it extends the commentary's prenatal-to-lifespan sequence across the body's emergence from the womb."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cfee4fe9c842ff5997a5", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 7:3", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "birth and common air", "note": "The speaker moves from womb formation to birth and breathing the common air. That transition is useful beside the commentary's formed body followed by divine breath and life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8eecb8ed0d601cdc1e8a", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 7:4", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "swaddling and infant care", "note": "Solomon says he was swaddled and cared for, giving a concrete childhood instance of the commentary's branch of Rabb as raising a child."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cbb952cb9017238ccd5e", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 7:5", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "king begins life like everyone", "note": "Even a king's birth has no different beginning; this Jewish reflection makes the commentary's bodily life stages universal rather than a lexical claim about one person."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c4ec14a5e3d4e5a7ef0e", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 7:6", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "one entrance and one exit", "note": "All people share one way into life and one way out; it frames the commentary's womb-to-old-age arc with mortality and a common human course."}

