{"connection_id":"BC-5c470ea53a4cffa8ed0d","ref":"SBLGNT:1Cor.15.43","status":"accepted","reason":"Weakness/power is the verse's explicit reversal and is stated in the annotation; it is not itself a claim about old age.","paragraphs":[23],"evidence":["SBLGNT:1Cor.15.35","SBLGNT:1Cor.15.36","SBLGNT:1Cor.15.37","SBLGNT:1Cor.15.38","SBLGNT:1Cor.15.42","SBLGNT:1Cor.15.43","SBLGNT:1Cor.15.44","SBLGNT:1Cor.15.45"],"annotations":["S087-INC-MTF-002"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-84f3dd84787418cfd308","ref":"SBLGNT:1Cor.15.44","status":"accepted","reason":"The natural/spiritual body distinction is explicit and represented as transformation in the linked annotation.","paragraphs":[23],"evidence":["SBLGNT:1Cor.15.35","SBLGNT:1Cor.15.36","SBLGNT:1Cor.15.37","SBLGNT:1Cor.15.38","SBLGNT:1Cor.15.42","SBLGNT:1Cor.15.43","SBLGNT:1Cor.15.44","SBLGNT:1Cor.15.45"],"annotations":["S087-INC-MTF-002"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-9015a836866d88626d5d","ref":"SBLGNT:1Cor.15.44","status":"accepted","reason":"Paul retains soma on both sides of the contrast; the annotation records a bodily resurrection distinction rather than disembodiment.","paragraphs":[23],"evidence":["SBLGNT:1Cor.15.35","SBLGNT:1Cor.15.36","SBLGNT:1Cor.15.37","SBLGNT:1Cor.15.38","SBLGNT:1Cor.15.42","SBLGNT:1Cor.15.43","SBLGNT:1Cor.15.44","SBLGNT:1Cor.15.45"],"annotations":["S087-INC-MTF-002"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-6863d686661be25d7a1b","ref":"SBLGNT:1Cor.15.45","status":"accepted","reason":"Paul explicitly contrasts the first living Adam with the last life-giving Adam; the annotation reports this Christian reading.","paragraphs":[23],"evidence":["SBLGNT:1Cor.15.35","SBLGNT:1Cor.15.36","SBLGNT:1Cor.15.37","SBLGNT:1Cor.15.38","SBLGNT:1Cor.15.42","SBLGNT:1Cor.15.43","SBLGNT:1Cor.15.44","SBLGNT:1Cor.15.45"],"annotations":["S087-INC-MTF-002"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-6772578508da3f130066","ref":"SBLGNT:1Cor.15.45","status":"accepted","reason":"The Christian Adam interpretation is explicit and included after the linked annotation's transformed-body argument.","paragraphs":[23],"evidence":["SBLGNT:1Cor.15.35","SBLGNT:1Cor.15.36","SBLGNT:1Cor.15.37","SBLGNT:1Cor.15.38","SBLGNT:1Cor.15.42","SBLGNT:1Cor.15.43","SBLGNT:1Cor.15.44","SBLGNT:1Cor.15.45"],"annotations":["S087-INC-MTF-002"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-4486494c91cde2b1b888","ref":"SBLGNT:1Cor.15.49","status":"rejected","reason":"The heavenly-image application follows the same bodily transformation already represented by 1 Corinthians 15:35-45 and adds no specific womb or pasture image at either paragraph.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:1Cor.15.49"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-ec362572eac0d0986022","ref":"SBLGNT:1John.2.17","status":"rejected","reason":"The passing world/enduring will contrast is valid but lacks the bodily and grass images that make Isaiah 40 and 1 Peter 1 directly useful beside ¶23.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:1John.2.17"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-665f3761689493f3bd0c","ref":"SBLGNT:1Pet.1.23","status":"accepted","reason":"New birth through imperishable seed and living word is explicit; the annotation connects it to the ensuing flesh/grass quotation.","paragraphs":[23],"evidence":["SBLGNT:1Pet.1.23","SBLGNT:1Pet.1.24","SBLGNT:1Pet.1.25"],"annotations":["S087-INC-YGL-001"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-7fb0d4449e8abadd4719","ref":"SBLGNT:1Pet.1.23","status":"accepted","reason":"The verse's imperishable seed and word ground the Christian new-birth reading stated in the annotation.","paragraphs":[23],"evidence":["SBLGNT:1Pet.1.23","SBLGNT:1Pet.1.24","SBLGNT:1Pet.1.25"],"annotations":["S087-INC-YGL-001"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-6172781f44bc95b52661","ref":"SBLGNT:1Pet.1.24","status":"accepted","reason":"The Greek quotes all flesh as grass and grass withering; the linked annotation explicitly names its Isaiah source.","paragraphs":[23],"evidence":["SBLGNT:1Pet.1.23","SBLGNT:1Pet.1.24","SBLGNT:1Pet.1.25"],"annotations":["S087-INC-YGL-001"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-3d3e7ab7534248bcfea2","ref":"SBLGNT:1Pet.1.24","status":"accepted","reason":"The flesh/grass line is an exact Christian citation of Isaiah's image and is quoted in the annotation.","paragraphs":[23],"evidence":["SBLGNT:1Pet.1.23","SBLGNT:1Pet.1.24","SBLGNT:1Pet.1.25"],"annotations":["S087-INC-YGL-001"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-87ef18586fd6206ce3b8","ref":"SBLGNT:1Pet.1.25","status":"accepted","reason":"The enduring word is explicitly identified with the proclaimed gospel; the annotation records that interpretive step.","paragraphs":[23],"evidence":["SBLGNT:1Pet.1.23","SBLGNT:1Pet.1.24","SBLGNT:1Pet.1.25"],"annotations":["S087-INC-YGL-001"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-b6d670c1cbab78a37cd7","ref":"SBLGNT:1Pet.1.3","status":"rejected","reason":"The verse's new birth is through Christ's resurrection, whereas the retained 1 Peter 1:23-25 passage specifically binds birth, seed, flesh, grass, and word to ¶23.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:1Pet.1.3"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-150abd4d2ba88ce7378c","ref":"SBLGNT:1Thess.4.16","status":"rejected","reason":"The trumpet and archangelic command belong to a future descent scene; equating that sound with Q 87:6's teaching/recitation exceeds the passage.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:1Thess.4.16"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-922cc4b08d0c631d493f","ref":"SBLGNT:1Thess.5.23","status":"rejected","reason":"Paul prays for spirit, soul, and body to be kept whole at Christ's coming; he does not describe fetal formation or a staged body.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:1Thess.5.23"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-b5a6f1f20e172dc970d5","ref":"SBLGNT:2Cor.4.16","status":"rejected","reason":"Outward decay and inward renewal are explicit, but this ethical duality supplies neither the prenatal stages of ¶22 nor the body/grass image of ¶23.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:2Cor.4.16"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-e787159349e3ff1d9f97","ref":"SBLGNT:2Cor.4.18","status":"rejected","reason":"Visible temporary versus unseen eternal is a broad contrast; Isaiah 40:6-8 and 1 Peter 1:23-25 supply ¶23's actual body and grass terms.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:2Cor.4.18"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-32121b8b09f4b81dd936","ref":"SBLGNT:2Cor.5.1","status":"rejected","reason":"Paul's earthly-tent metaphor addresses future dwelling, not the formed body or drying pasture that these two paragraphs analyze.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:2Cor.5.1"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-0c72cd4a8f23470c2a5d","ref":"SBLGNT:Acts.17.25","status":"rejected","reason":"God giving all people life and breath is genuine, but Genesis 2:7 and Ezekiel 37 provide the concrete formed-body/breath sequence used at ¶23.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Acts.17.25"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-52b62e07604832ff1d21","ref":"SBLGNT:Acts.17.26","status":"rejected","reason":"The allotted times and boundaries are those of nations' habitation; using them as a textual parallel for fetal bodily proportion would change the referent.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Acts.17.26"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-d5cac63a99cfdac2fc9f","ref":"SBLGNT:Eph.4.13","status":"rejected","reason":"The 'mature man' and measure describe the church's unity and Christlike fullness, not an individual's physical maturation in ¶22.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Eph.4.13"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-966e744b2f784692513b","ref":"SBLGNT:Gal.1.15","status":"rejected","reason":"Paul's being set apart from his mother's womb concerns vocation, not the body's prenatal shaping; Psalm 139 and Isaiah 44 give the closer formation wording.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Gal.1.15"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-787f3f955f546c5758d6","ref":"SBLGNT:Heb.11.11","status":"rejected","reason":"Sarah's conception beyond childbearing age is a particular promised-birth episode, whereas ¶22's old-age root note is a general life stage.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Heb.11.11"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-32422929d68927920e00","ref":"SBLGNT:Heb.5.13","status":"rejected","reason":"Milk-fed infancy here figures an inexperienced learner; Luke 2:40,52 supplies the literal bodily growth point of ¶22.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Heb.5.13"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-5303782034a20e8413f5","ref":"SBLGNT:Heb.5.14","status":"rejected","reason":"Maturity is trained moral discernment, not bodily completion, and does not add to ¶23's resurrection or grass motif.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Heb.5.14"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-d9ad4327ce8bb334d9f4","ref":"SBLGNT:Jas.1.10","status":"rejected","reason":"The rich person's grass-flower fate adds a wealth application; ¶23's explicit flesh/grass formulation is already shown from Isaiah 40 and 1 Peter 1.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Jas.1.10"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-2c8d15779801ca907c3d","ref":"SBLGNT:Jas.1.11","status":"rejected","reason":"James uses the scorched grass to warn a rich person in his pursuits; its ethical application is narrower than ¶23's body-and-vegetation claim, directly supplied by Isaiah 40.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Jas.1.11"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-e276c81588e2800bb221","ref":"SBLGNT:Jas.4.14","status":"rejected","reason":"A passing vapor conveys brevity of life but lacks either the formed body or the grass/pasture image that anchors this section.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Jas.4.14"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-09ca2ba4ba55242e160d","ref":"SBLGNT:John.16.21","status":"rejected","reason":"The labor-pain comparison explains a turn from sorrow to joy, not Arabic q-r-ʾ's lexical branches or God's shaping of a fetus.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:John.16.21"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-7eb8d71af31774d8ea4c","ref":"SBLGNT:John.3.4","status":"accepted","reason":"Nicodemus explicitly imagines entering his mother's womb again; the linked annotation contrasts literal and figurative birth.","paragraphs":[22],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Luke.2.40","SBLGNT:Luke.2.52","SBLGNT:John.3.4","SBLGNT:John.3.5"],"annotations":["S087-INC-MTF-001"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-c643e75b472d2f9f4833","ref":"SBLGNT:John.3.5","status":"accepted","reason":"Jesus answers with birth from water and Spirit, as the linked annotation's figurative-birth contrast states; it is not a Qur'anic counter-narrative.","paragraphs":[22],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Luke.2.40","SBLGNT:Luke.2.52","SBLGNT:John.3.4","SBLGNT:John.3.5"],"annotations":["S087-INC-MTF-001"]}
{"connection_id":"BC-e67885a1ab8792aad8e5","ref":"SBLGNT:John.5.28","status":"rejected","reason":"Tombs hearing Jesus' voice concerns final resurrection; the proposed bridge to Q 87:6's recitation treats unlike speakers and settings as one sound motif.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:John.5.28"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-cc84baa4ca1ca4ee6fcd","ref":"SBLGNT:John.5.29","status":"rejected","reason":"Resurrection into life or judgment is clear, but this verse has no creation, gestation, or vegetation element beyond the resurrection horizon supplied more closely by 1 Corinthians 15.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:John.5.29"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-b341673cc3e028eee57d","ref":"SBLGNT:Luke.1.15","status":"rejected","reason":"John's Spirit-filled prenatal vocation is a distinct birth story; it does not describe the physical form or life-course progression that ¶22 assembles.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Luke.1.15"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-98a8164c9efdd298975a","ref":"SBLGNT:Luke.1.36","status":"rejected","reason":"Elizabeth's pregnancy in old age is a special reversal of barrenness, not an ordinary sequence from prenatal shaping to senescence.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Luke.1.36"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-e45155e4a31a21660d7f","ref":"SBLGNT:Luke.1.41","status":"rejected","reason":"The infant's leap at Mary's greeting is fetal motion in a visitation scene, not a description of God's fashioning or proportioning the body.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Luke.1.41"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-3aa24caad80fb2352f79","ref":"SBLGNT:Luke.1.41","status":"rejected","reason":"The same leap is a narrated response during visitation; calling it a parallel to the creation/measure sequence would overstate the shared point.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Luke.1.41"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-54e9cc89989b2d56f225","ref":"SBLGNT:Luke.12.20","status":"rejected","reason":"The rich planner's sudden death matches the later ruined-garden warning, but does not specify the bodily formation or body/grass image for which ¶23 has closer sources.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Luke.12.20"],"annotations":[]}
{"connection_id":"BC-b4a82eafcebfc7949b11","ref":"SBLGNT:Luke.2.40","status":"accepted","reason":"The verse describes a child's actual growth and strengthening, which the annotation distinguishes from figurative rebirth.","paragraphs":[22],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Luke.2.40","SBLGNT:Luke.2.52","SBLGNT:John.3.4","SBLGNT:John.3.5"],"annotations":["S087-INC-MTF-001"]}

